<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Theban Revolt</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/206-bce">206</a>–186 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Theban Revolt was an uprising in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic dynasty</a>, lasting from 206 to 186 BCE. Driven by heavy taxes and a devalued currency after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a>, Theban autonomist feeling and anti-Greek sentiment, the rebels seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-edfu">Temple of Edfu</a> and Thebes, and their leader was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> as Haronnophris in 205 BCE. Ptolemy V's army retook Thebes in 199 BCE, but a successor, Chaonnophris, fought on until his defeat south of Aswan in 186 BCE and his execution at Memphis. The revolt pushed later Ptolemies to court the Egyptian clergy and play the role of pharaoh, though a Greco-Macedonian elite still dominated.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-i-soter">Ptolemy I Soter</a> founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> in 305 BCE, and his successors were crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> and presented their state as the heir of pharaonic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, restoring temples and courting the clergy. Yet the state ran on Greek lines, with strong military control and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> shut out of positions of responsibility. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-syrian-war">Fourth Syrian War</a> the mass demobilisation of soldiers unsettled society as well. The Greek historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, a contemporary, said the rising was led by Egyptian soldiers returning from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-raphia">Battle of Raphia</a>, an account most historians now reject.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The rebels fortified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-edfu">Temple of Edfu</a>, a symbol of Ptolemaic power, and drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. The rising spread because Ptolemaic power was suddenly weakened: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> died unexpectedly, the regent Queen Arsinoe III was murdered by her advisers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> rioted and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>. Haronnophris ruled from at least Abydos to Per-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a>, revived old pharaonic titles and offices, and had Greek buildings such as the public baths at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> destroyed. The kings of Kush at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a>, Arqamani and then Adikhalamani, took the lands south of Aswan and seem to have backed the rebels to the end.</p><p>In 199 BCE the rebels lost Abydos after a long siege and were defeated near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coptos">Coptos</a>. Haronnophris vanished and Chaonnophris appeared; most historians think he was a successor, though some evidence suggests one man changed his name. Ptolemy V was crowned at Memphis, where the decree preserved on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone</a> was issued, but Chaonnophris regained the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebaid">Thebaid</a> around 197 BCE and pushed north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asyut">Asyut</a>, scene of heavy fighting from 195 to 191 BCE. A general named Ptolemaios retook Thebes in 191 BCE, and his successor Komanos, probably an epistrategos, defeated Chaonnophris on 27 August 186 BCE; the rebel <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s son and the Nubian leaders died in the battle.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The revolt stands out among Egyptian risings against the Ptolemies for its length, its spread across much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a> and its leaders' use of the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>. The recovery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> brought reforms meant to associate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> more closely with the state rather than bloody reprisals, and the priests, whose part in the revolt is uncertain, were not punished. It was the clearest sign within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> of the collapse of Ptolemaic power at the end of the 3rd century BCE.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Great Theban Revolt” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At home, the <b>Great Theban Revolt</b> was still under way when Ptolemy V was crowned at Memphis at the age of 12.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
